+L72: # Nonsingular, big-bounce cosmology from spinor-torsion coupling
+L73: 
+L74: cite16†Nikodem Popławski L75: 
+L76:   * ^{}Department of Physics, Indiana University, Bloomington, Indiana, USA*
+L77: 
+L78:   * ^{*}nikodem.poplawski@gmail.com
+L79: 
+L80: cite17†PDF L81: 
+L82: Phys. Rev. D 85, 107502 – Published 29 May, 2012
+L83: 
+L84: DOI: https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.85.107502
+L85: 
+L86: [Button: Export Citation]
+L87: 
+L88: Show metrics
+L89: ## Abstract
+L90: The Einstein-Cartan-Sciama-Kibble theory of gravity removes the constraint of general relativity that the affine connection be symmetric by regarding its antisymmetric part, the torsion tensor, as a dynamical variable. The minimal coupling between the torsion tensor and Dirac spinors generates a spin-spin interaction which is significant in fermionic matter at extremely high densities.
+L91: We show that such an interaction averts the unphysical big-bang singularity, replacing it with a cusp-like bounce at a finite minimum scale factor, before which the Universe was contracting. This scenario also explains why the present Universe at largest scales appears spatially flat, homogeneous and isotropic.
